fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1079", description: some(value: "A SKOLEM HAS NO REFLECT REPRESENTATION, AND `TypeExtractor.TypeVar` CANNOT CARRY RIGIDITY — the flex/rigid distinction the typer runs on is invisible to the reflect layer.\n\nWI-1063 opens a return's existential slot to a fresh skolem at each use, minted by\n`UnwrittenFill::Anonymous` as a bare `Term::Var(Var::Rigid(vid))`. MEASURED by\nconstructing both carriers and calling `extract_type`:\n\n  Term::Var(Var::Rigid(vid))    ->  TypeExtractor::Error\n  Term::Var(Var::Global(vid))   ->  TypeExtractor::Error\n\n`type_head` classifies on the FUNCTOR SYMBOL (`let Some(f) = head.functor_sym() else {\nreturn TypeHead::Error }`), and a bare variable term has none. So a skolem — a\nperfectly well-formed type, an opaque constant — is reported as a MALFORMED TERM, and\n`TypeExtractor::Error(term)`'s own doc calls its payload \"the offending input\". The\n`extract` totality claim (\"keeps `extract` total rather than returning an `Option`\") is\nsatisfied in the letter and broken in the spirit: total, but with the wrong answer.\n\nTWO REPRESENTATIONS OF ONE CONCEPT, and only one of them is reflectable:\n\n  * ENGINE:  `Term::Var(Var::Global | Var::Rigid)` — what unification and the WI-1063\n    opening actually manipulate. `Var::Rigid`'s own doc names it: \"Equivalent to\n    'Skolem constant' (resolution literature) or 'eigenvariable' (sequent calculus).\"\n  * REFLECT: `TypeExtractor.TypeVar(name: Symbol)` — a deep entity minted by\n    `make_type_var`, carrying A NAME AND NOTHING ELSE.\n\nTHE FIELD THAT IS MISSING IS RIGIDITY, and that is the finding rather than the mere\n`Error`. `TypeVar(name: Symbol)` has no flex/rigid discriminator, so converting a\nskolem into one instead of erroring would still LOSE what makes it a skolem: a reflect\nconsumer cannot tell an OPAQUE CONSTANT (unifies with nothing but itself) from a\nUNIFIABLE VARIABLE (binds to anything). That is the same conflation WI-1078 is about —\na named `?E` escaping the existential opening — expressed one level down, in the data\nmodel rather than in the rule.\n\nWHO IS AFFECTED, to be established rather than assumed — the scope question this ticket\nmust answer FIRST, and the reason it is a ticket rather than an inline fix:\n\n  (a) Does a skolem ever REACH `extract` today? A negative is hard to prove; the\n      honest form is to make the `Error` arm LOUD for a variable carrier (a distinct\n      diagnostic naming the var and its rigidity) and run all seven corpus tiers plus\n      the suite. Silence is then evidence of unreachability, and a hit names its site.\n      Note the repo's own rule: prefer a loud error over a silent skip.\n  (b) `anthill-cpp-gen` calls `extract_type` directly (`lib.rs:1814`, `:4760`) and\n      matches on the variants; what does it do with `Error` today?\n  (c) The reflect-layer typing rules in `stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill` read\n      types through this enum. Any of them reasoning about a return's type would see\n      `Error` where a skolem stands.\n\nTHE BINDER LAYER IS ABSENT ENTIRELY, which decides how the options below relate.\n`OperationInfo` (reflect.anthill) carries name, params, return_type, effects, requires,\nensures, meta — and NO `type_params`. An operation's bracket binders (`map[Dst, EffP]`)\nare not reflected at all. So \"add a quantified type\" is not extending a partial\nrepresentation; it is introducing the binder layer.\n\nTHE TWO DESIGNS ARE NOT PEERS (user's question, 2026-08-11: \"introduce Rigid-Vars, or\nPolyType with bounded TypeVars?\"). A rigid-var form is a COMPONENT of any design with\nquantifiers, not a rival to one — opening an existential, or entering a universal's\nbody, always yields an opaque constant that has to be representable (System F has both\nLambda-bound and free variables). So RIGID-VARS IS NEEDED EITHER WAY and can land first.\nWhat it does NOT give is the quantified form: with rigid-vars alone the reflect layer\ncan say \"this is opaque\" but not \"this type is forall A. List[A]\", so `empty`'s\npolymorphism still has no representation and keeps surviving by escape hatches (the\nself-sort tie for `List.empty`, the named variable for `LogicalStream.empty` — WI-1078).\n\nA POLYTYPE WITH BOUNDED TYPEVARS IS THE DESIGN THAT RETIRES THE GUESSING, and it\ncollapses three open tickets into one question:\n\n  WI-1077  carrier parameter INFERRED from operation shapes (four separate\n           inferences of one undeclared fact)      -> a BOUNDED binder `A: Iterable`\n                                                      says it\n  WI-1078  bound-vs-unbound named `?E` decided by SPELLING (named vs anonymous)\n                                                   -> structural: is the variable in\n                                                      the binder list?\n  WI-1079  rigidity unrepresentable                -> the binder carries its\n                                                      quantifier; opening discharges\n                                                      it to a rigid\n\nThat is the user's \"specify the quantor in the type\" (WI-1078's option 3) given a home\nin the data model, and it addresses the root cause the three tickets are symptoms of:\nthe language keeps INFERRING what it never SAYS. Cost is correspondingly larger — every\n`TypeExtractor` reader, `anthill-cpp-gen`'s direct `extract_type` matches, and the new\nbinder layer on `OperationInfo`.\n\nRECOMMENDED ORDER: rigid-vars first (local, necessary under either design, closes this\nticket's measured defect and gives WI-1078 somewhere to put its answer); PolyType as the\ndesign target, taken as its own ticket with its own measurement, and only if the surface\ngains explicit quantifiers — a binder list in reflect that no syntax can write would be\na second undeclared fact rather than a fix.\n\nTHE SHAPE, decided with the user 2026-08-11 and grounded in what the language already\nspells. Three questions were asked and they collapse into one answer.\n\n  Q: should a PolyType carry ONE quantifier, or a set of (variable, quantifier) pairs?\n  Q: or can we assume that if we are in a PolyType, it is FORALL?\n  A: ASSUME FORALL, and store no quantifier at all. In anthill every binder is attached\n     to a DECLARATION, never to a free-floating type — `operation map[Dst, EffP](…)`,\n     `sort [F] { … }`, `sort T = ?`. There is no position in which a mixed binder list\n     could arise, and §5.6 already says an operation type parameter is \"the CALLER's to\n     instantiate\", which is forall by construction. Storing a per-binder quantifier\n     would additionally make an ILLEGAL STATE REPRESENTABLE: the polarity rule makes the\n     quantifier a function of POSITION (parameter = forall, return = exists), so a\n     binder tagged exists could sit in a forall position and the two would disagree.\n     Store what is NOT derivable — the name and the BOUND.\n\n  THE EXISTENTIAL NEEDS NO BINDER NODE. At a declaration it is implied by the return\n  position; at a use it has ALREADY BEEN OPENED into a rigid, which is exactly the\n  Skolem variant this ticket is about. The one candidate exception is `ensures Spec[C]`\n  (WI-402), the EXPLICIT existential the loader rewrites away into a bare return — an\n  `Exists` node is what would let that spelling survive instead of buying nothing\n  (WI-1063's words).\n\n  A BINDER CARRIES NO BOUND, corrected 2026-08-11 after an earlier draft of this note\n  proposed `TypeVarDecl(name, bound)`. CHECKED IN THE GRAMMAR: `operation_type_param` is\n  `seq(field('name', identifier), optional(seq('=', field('default', _type))))`, and the\n  `= default` arm is parsed ONLY to give a good diagnostic — WI-850 refuses it, and\n  kernel-language §5.4 says `TypeParam ::= Name`. `sort_type_param`'s optional list is\n  the HIGHER-KINDED member list (`F[T, …]`), not a bound either. So NEITHER binder form\n  can carry one, and a `bound` field would be permanently empty.\n\n  BOUNDS EXIST ONLY AT SORT LEVEL AND ARE ALREADY REFLECTED: a sort's `requires Eq[T]`\n  (and the `effects E` desugaring, `sort E = ? requires EffectsRuntime[Effects = E]`) is\n  a SEPARATE clause, emitted as `SortRequiresInfo`. It is not part of the binder and must\n  not be duplicated into one.\n\n  POLYTYPE IS A FORM OF TYPE, NOT A FIELD ON AN OPERATION (user, 2026-08-11, correcting\n  an earlier draft of this ticket that proposed `OperationInfo(…, type_params:\n  List[Symbol])`). It is a `TypeExtractor` VARIANT, sitting beside `Arrow`,\n  `Parameterized` and `NamedTuple`:\n\n    entity PolyType(binders: List[Symbol], body: Term)      -- forall by construction\n    entity Skolem(id: Symbol)                               -- an OPENED existential\n    entity FlexVar(id: Symbol)                              -- an INSTANTIATED forall\n    (optional) entity Exists(binders: List[Symbol], body: Term)\n                                                            -- only to stop `ensures\n                                                               Spec[C]` being erased\n\n    empty : PolyType([A], Arrow(NamedTuple([]), List[A], {}, 0))\n\n  THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT COSMETIC, and the eta-lift is the proof. A field on\n  `OperationInfo` can only ever describe an OPERATION; a type FORM composes — it appears\n  anywhere a type can. `typing.rs:6271` excludes a type-parameterized operation from\n  having a function-value form because \"its arrow would carry unfreshened type-param vars\n  that alias across multiple eta-lifts of the same op\". A `type_params` FIELD could not\n  fix that at all: a function VALUE has no `OperationInfo`, it has a TYPE. Only a\n  polymorphic type form can be that value's type, with freshening at instantiation.\n\n  SO `OperationInfo` PROBABLY NEEDS NO NEW FIELD. Its type IS the PolyType, and\n  `params` / `return_type` / `effects` are a decomposition of that type's body. Whether\n  the info record should keep the decomposition, carry the assembled type, or both, is a\n  question for the implementation — but \"add type_params to OperationInfo\" is NOT this\n  ticket's deliverable and an earlier draft was wrong to state it as one.\n\n  AN OPERATION IS A FUNCTION FROM A NAMED TUPLE (user, 2026-08-11), and the reflect\n  layer already says so: `TypeExtractor.Arrow(param, result, effects, arity)`, where\n  WI-791's rule is \"at arity one `param` is the sole parameter's TYPE, at any other\n  arity it is the parameter LIST — a `NamedTuple` of exactly `arity` components\". So an\n  operation's TYPE is already an arrow over a named tuple, and `OperationInfo`'s\n  separate `params` / `return_type` / `effects` fields are a DECOMPOSITION of one type\n  rather than three independent facts.\n\n  THAT REFRAMING HAS A MEASURED PAYOFF, and it changes which target is worth building.\n  An arrow has nowhere to put binders, and the consequence is a user-visible capability\n  gap rather than a representation nicety — `typing.rs:6271`, in the eta-lift gate:\n\n      // Only a type-parameterized op stays excluded: its arrow would carry unfreshened\n      // type-param vars that alias across multiple eta-lifts of the same op.\n      if !op.type_params.is_empty() { return None; }\n\n  So EVERY type-parameterized stdlib operation — `Iterable.map`, `find`, `filter`,\n  `FiniteCollection.foldLeft`, the finite-combinator `collect`/`iterator` — CANNOT BE\n  PASSED AS A FUNCTION VALUE. WI-1063 recorded the same fact from the other end (\"an\n  arrow type has nowhere to write exists; it is why an operation with type parameters\n  has no function-value form either\") and its eta site opens once per LIFT rather than\n  once per application for the same reason.\n\n  WHICH MEANS THE MINIMAL SHAPE ABOVE IS NOT ENOUGH TO BUY ANYTHING. A\n  `type_params: List[Symbol]` field RECORDS the binders but puts them nowhere the arrow\n  can use, so the aliasing that forces the exclusion is untouched and the restriction\n  stays. `PolyType(binders, Arrow(NamedTuple(params), result, effects, arity))` is the\n  shape where per-use instantiation freshens the binders, the aliasing disappears, and\n  the eta gate's second clause can be RETIRED — which is a capability delivered, not a\n  field added. Scope it as: the minimal field closes this ticket's measured defect\n  (reflect cannot see the binders); the PolyType wrap is the increment that lifts the\n  function-value restriction, and it should carry that restriction's removal as its\n  acceptance rather than being justified by tidiness.\n\n  WHERE THE BINDER SITS DECIDES `empty`'s RESULT TYPE (user's question, 2026-08-11).\n  Under a PolyType the quantifier is on the ARROW, not on the return:\n\n      empty : forall A. () -> List[A]\n            = PolyType([A], Arrow(NamedTuple([]), List[A], {}, 0))\n\n  so the result type is `List[A]` with `A` bound by the enclosing forall, and each USE\n  instantiates it freshly. That is the fresh-per-use behaviour already measured, but\n  DECLARED instead of emergent — and it collapses the three different mechanisms the\n  corpus currently relies on into one:\n\n    mk() -> List            at namespace level   REFUSED  (no binder: the return's\n                                                           unwritten slot is exists,\n                                                           opened to a rigid — correct)\n    List.empty() -> List    inside `sort List`   works    (self-sort tie: the slot is\n                                                           List's own T)\n    LogicalStream.empty()\n      -> LogicalStream[?A]                       works    (named-variable exemption —\n                                                           WI-1078's hole)\n\n  THE `Nothing` OPTION ANSWERS THE SAME QUESTION DIFFERENTLY, and for a NULLARY producer\n  it may be the better answer. Both measured:\n\n    forall A     `mk3[A]() -> List[T = A]` loads and serves Int64 and String in one\n                 program. COST: where nothing pins A the call is a loud \"expected a type\n                 for 'A', got unconstrained — use `mk[A = …](…)`\", so `empty()` with no\n                 expectation in scope is an error.\n    List[Nothing]  `mk6() -> List[T = Nothing]` loads. The result type is CONCRETE, so it\n                 never needs pinning; usability comes from `Covariant(sort: List, param:\n                 T)` by subtyping.\n\n  For an EMPTY collection the second is arguably more honest — there is a canonical\n  element type, the uninhabited one, so nothing needs inferring; the forall version says\n  \"any A\" and then demands the context supply one for a value with no elements to\n  disagree about. The tradeoff is which machinery pays: forall needs instantiation,\n  `Nothing` needs variance. NOT MEASURED, and the thing to check before choosing: whether\n  the declared `Covariant` fact is actually ENFORCED at this position, or only recorded.\n\n  WHAT THIS DOES NOT GIVE WI-1077, stated because the earlier draft implied it did: a\n  bounded binder is NOT delivered here, so \"let a declaration say which parameter is the\n  carrier\" still needs NEW SURFACE (`sort C = ? carrier`, or a `spec` keyword — WI-1077's\n  own options (a)/(b)). The WI-1077 -> WI-1079 dependency still holds, but for the weaker\n  reason: this ticket builds the binder LAYER that such a marker would attach to and be\n  reflected through, not the marker.\n\n  SYNTAX, asked and answered: `[A]List[A]` COLLIDES. grammar.js's collection_literal\n  says it in as many words — \"Bare [...] = collection literal, Name[...] = application\n  (disambiguated by leading Name)\" — and since types are terms the two share one\n  grammar, so a leading bare `[A]` parses as a collection containing `A`. Every binder\n  the language has is introduced by a KEYWORD, never bare. A free-standing quantified\n  type would therefore need a keyword prefix — but it may not be wanted at all, since\n  fixing `OperationInfo.type_params` IS most of PolyType and needs NO new syntax: the\n  binders are already written, merely not reflected.\n\nTHE SCOPE QUESTION IS SETTLED, AND NOT BY A REACHABILITY MEASUREMENT (user, 2026-08-11:\n\"we should represent all in reflect layer. Its why it exists\"). An earlier draft of this\nticket made \"does a skolem ever REACH `extract` today?\" the first task, with a loud arm\nand a seven-tier run as the instrument. That is the wrong gate: the reflect layer's job\nIS to represent the language's types, so a form it cannot express is a defect whether or\nnot today's corpus reaches it. Reachability may still be worth knowing for PRIORITY; it\nis not the acceptance.\n\nAND THE GAP IS DEMONSTRABLY NOT HYPOTHETICAL — WI-1076 IS ITS BUG. A declared type\nPARAMETER appears in reflect as `SortRef(T)`, indistinguishable from a concrete sort:\nthat is exactly why `List provides Stream[T]` filed its carrier at `T`, because\n`provision_carrier_binding` filters the binding's base on `SymbolKind::Sort` and a type\nparameter PASSES that filter. So the conflation already shipped a defect, one ticket ago.\n\nTHREE FORMS NEED REPRESENTING, and only the first is a new construct:\n\n  a BOUND variable        `forall A. () -> List[A]`   no binder exists anywhere today,\n                                                     and `A` in the return reads as\n                                                     `SortRef(A)`            -> PolyType\n                                                                                + bound-var\n  an INSTANTIATED FLEX    `?A1`, the type of          `TypeExtractor::Error`  -> flex-var\n                          `empty()` at a use          (MEASURED)                 form\n  an OPENED RIGID         `rho`, the type of          `TypeExtractor::Error`  -> skolem\n                          `widen(s)` at a use         (MEASURED)                 form\n\nPolyType covers the first and gives the other two a provenance: INSTANTIATING a PolyType\nyields the flex, OPENING an existential yields the rigid. That is why it is the chosen\ndirection rather than three unrelated variants — the three forms are one lifecycle, and\nrepresenting them as one keeps a reader from having to reconstruct which is which.\n\nOPTIONS FOR THE FIRST STEP, smallest first:\n  1. ADD A FIELD — `TypeVar(name: Symbol, rigid: Bool)`. Smallest, but touches every\n     construction site of `make_type_var` and every reader that matches `TypeVar`.\n  2. ADD A VARIANT — `entity Skolem(id: Symbol)` beside `TypeVar`, and a `type_head`\n     arm for the bare-variable carrier. Keeps `TypeVar`'s readers untouched and makes\n     the two genuinely different things different, which is this repo's stated\n     preference (make illegal state unrepresentable over check logic).\n  3. DECIDE SKOLEMS NEVER REACH REFLECT and say so at `extract_type`, with (a)'s\n     measurement as the evidence and a loud arm to keep it true.\n\nOption 2 also gives WI-1078 somewhere to put its answer: if a named `?E` in a return\nopens, the thing it opens TO needs a name in the reflect layer.\n\nACCEPTANCE: (a) is measured and reported — whether a skolem reaches `extract` in any\ncorpus tier, counted at the site rather than argued; the chosen option is implemented\nand `extract_type` on a `Var::Rigid` no longer answers `Error` (or the decision that it\nmay is written at the function with the measurement behind it); a test DRIVES the\ndistinction — a skolem and a flexible variable must extract to different things, which\nis the assertion that fails today for both directions at once; kernel-language's\n§\"In a RETURN the quantifier flips to exists\" says how an opened slot is represented,\nsince it currently describes the semantics and not the datum; each test says which case\nfails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1063 (the opening, and `UnwrittenFill::Anonymous` which mints the skolem),\nWI-1078 (the same flex/rigid conflation at the rule level), WI-1061/WI-1059 (the\nprojection filler, the one skolem that DOES have a reflect form — `ExprCarried`, because\nthe language spells `s.T`), WI-428 (`RigidTypeProjection`, a projection off a rigid\nsubject, which is NOT a rigid variable), WI-540 (the `TypeExtractor` sort), WI-391 (the\n\"every backing term classifies into exactly one structural variant\" invariant this\nticket says is unmet for a variable carrier)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-11T22:53:13Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1079", name: "quantifiers")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1079", author: "claude", content: "SCOPED TO THE FORMS; POLYTYPE SPLIT OUT (user, 2026-08-11). This ticket delivers the three VARIABLE FORMS in the reflect layer — a bound variable, an INSTANTIATED flex, an OPENED rigid — via option 2 (new TypeExtractor variants), which is its own measured defect and needs no new syntax. PolyType becomes its own ticket, WI-1083, whose acceptance is RETIRING THE ETA GATE rather than tidiness.\n\nWHY THE SPLIT GOT SHARPER: this ticket argued PolyType by \"it collapses three open tickets into one question\". Two of those three legs are now gone. WI-1078 is DELIVERED without PolyType (a signature scan). WI-1077 is answered (c) — leave the carrier inferred, no marker, no binder — so its dependency on this ticket has been REMOVED. What survives as a capability rather than a nicety is the eta gate alone (typing.rs:6274, \"if !op.type_params.is_empty() { return None; }\"), which is why WI-1083 carries that as its acceptance.\n\nOPTION 1 IS WORSE THAN THIS TICKET SAYS, and the reason is worth recording before the choice is made again. \"Add a field, TypeVar(name, rigid: Bool)\" assumes TypeVar IS the variable form. MEASURED at its producers: kb.make_type_var is minted for \"?param\" (an un-annotated lambda binder, typing.rs:7992/8639/10062/10223/10268) and for \"?_\" (an UN-LOWERABLE carrier, typing.rs:40669/40675) — it is the PLACEHOLDER form, \"a type we could not name\". A declared type parameter reads as SortRef(T); an engine variable (Term::Var(Global|Rigid)) reads as Error. So option 1 would bolt rigidity onto a construct that never represented a variable at all, and the two questions (\"is this a hole in the type\" vs \"is this a variable, and is it opaque\") would share one name — the defect class this repo names most often.\n\nWI-1078 HANDS THIS TICKET A QUESTION IT DOES NOT YET ASK: WHO GENERALIZES. WI-1078 implemented \"bound\" as a scan over THREE sources — parameter types, requires clauses, and the op own [A] binders. A PolyType binder list is only the THIRD. mplus(a: LogicalStream[?A], b: LogicalStream[?A]) -> LogicalStream[?A] declares no brackets, so its type_params is EMPTY and ?A is a free logical variable shared across the signature. MEASURED, not inferred: the params-dropped back-out (which leaves requires + type_params intact) failed exactly the a_signature_bound_variable_is_left_flexible row. So a structural reading of \"bound iff the variable is in the binder list\" would classify mplus ?A as UNBOUND and open it, refusing a program that loads today. PolyType construction must therefore GENERALIZE a signature free logical variables into binders, and this ticket nowhere says who does that or when — at load, at reflection, or at instantiation. Whoever takes WI-1083 must answer it first; it is the difference between PolyType subsuming WI-1078 rule and contradicting it.\n\nTHE SCOPE QUESTION STAYS SETTLED as recorded above (\"we should represent all in reflect layer. Its why it exists\") — reachability is priority information, not acceptance.", at: "2026-08-11T21:51:45Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1079", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED as the FORMS half (option 2, new variants); PolyType is WI-1083.\n\nTHE SCOPE QUESTION (a) IS ANSWERED AND THE ANSWER IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT THIS TICKET EXPECTED. It asked whether a skolem ever REACHES extract today, calling a negative \"hard to prove\". Counted at type_head Error bail ON MAIN, a bare stdlib load classifies a logical variable as a malformed term 4911 times — 3503 rigid, 1408 flex; the anthill-todo tier adds more (6414 flex). The defect was live in enormous volume, and it broke a criterion recorded on WI-391 a ticket before it existed (\"TypeExtractor.Error is reserved for malformed USER input, never a system-minted shape\" — a skolem is minted by rigidify_op_type_params).\n\nWHAT LANDED: entity FlexVar(name, id) and entity Skolem(name, id) in the stdlib TypeExtractor enum, mirrored as TypeHead/TypeExtractor variants, classified by a new ViewHead::Var arm in type_head and reified by extract_type_builtin. OPTION 1 (a rigid: Bool field on TypeVar) WAS REJECTED ON A MEASUREMENT, not on taste: TypeVar is not the variable. Its producers are ?param (an un-annotated lambda binder) and ?_ (an un-lowerable carrier, typing.rs:40669); a DECLARED type parameter reads as SortRef(T). It is the PLACEHOLDER form, so a flag would have given one entity two questions.\n\nid IS THE IDENTITY AND name IS NOT — two skolems of one parameter name are different types that render alike. Every test row that builds two variables builds them from the same name, so nothing passes on rendering.\n\nDE BRUIJN STAYS Error, stated rather than omitted: it is a RULE binder, opened to a fresh Global before anything type-checks. NO DISPATCH TAG either — type_dispatch_name_view still answers None for both, deliberately, because that tag routes the STRUCTURAL unify/subtype arms which a variable never reaches; naming it would change typer routing, which this scope excludes.\n\nA SIMPLIFICATION FELL OUT, and completing it took two passes. value_contains_rigid opened with two hand-rolled carrier arms testing Var::Rigid because extract_type could not answer; deleted. REVIEW THEN FOUND THE SAME DUPLICATE FOR THE FLEX HALF in value_flex_var_id and value_is_anonymous_wildcard — and it was NARROWER, not merely redundant: no Value::Node arm, while an occurrence carrying Expr::Var(Global) heads as ViewHead::Var. One-directional and silent, so an anonymous ? in a slot riding the Node carrier answered \"not a variable\" and the WI-1063/WI-1078 opening did not fire for that spelling. Both now ask extract_type.\n\nMEASURED: the back-out (deleting the ViewHead::Var arm) costs 15 rows — 2 in the new file, the rewritten WI-963 pin that held the OLD verdict, and TWELVE from value_contains_rigid callers across WI-1013/219/732/759/946, which is what makes the deletion safe rather than tidy. Downstream readers CHECKED not assumed: anthill-cpp-gen matches Arrow/NamedTuple with a _ fallthrough into the same error path Error took (no change), and NO stdlib rule cases over TypeExtractor at all — they use extract_sort_ref, which answers question (c). All six corpus tiers load at +6 facts each, exactly the two new entities. scaland green (461) since sort.anthill is in the Scala bootstrap compiled closure.\n\nTWO INSTRUMENT FAILURES, both producing clean plausible numbers, both recorded in the test header: a per-tier CLI loop run from the wrong working directory reported 0 hits for every tier (the binary did not exist and grep -c counted an empty stream); and a cargo test census without --nocapture sees only FAILING tests output. The suite millions were the positive control that exposed the first.\n\nALSO CORRECTED: make_type_var doc in kb/mod.rs still asserted the rule this ticket reversed while naming the rewritten wi963 test as its driver; and wi391_binding_extractability_test guard is now narrower (a raw Var::Global in a binding position used to trip its Error assertion, now passes as FlexVar) — noted at the assertion so a future leak of that class is not read as covered.\n\nREMAINING FOR WI-1083: the BOUND variable of a forall has no form — it needs the binder that introduces it. And the generalization question recorded there (WI-1078 bound is a scan over THREE sources; a binder list is only one) is still the first thing that ticket must answer.", at: "2026-08-11T22:53:15Z")
